// Duration literals, and the `hold` builtin that waits for one.
//
// A duration is a number with a unit stuck to it: h for hours, m for minutes, s
// for seconds. Several can run together with nothing between them, and a trailing
// number with no unit of its own is seconds. So the three ways of writing the same
// wait are `37`, `37s` and `0h0m37`, and `hold(2h21m37)` is `hold(8497)`.
//
// That is the whole design: the literal is an ordinary integer count of seconds,
// so there is no duration type for the parser or the code generator to know about,
// and a duration composes in arithmetic like anything else. Printing one shows the
// seconds, which is deliberate -- it is what makes `hold(2h)` and `hold(7200)`
// visibly the same call.
//
// This test prints every spelling rather than waiting for it. The waits are all
// zero except for a single `hold(1)`, because a golden case that slept for real
// would add its duration to every run of the suite at every optimization level,
// and the literals are the part with the interesting edges. The one second is
// here so that the runtime call is exercised end to end rather than only its
// argument checking.

int main() {
    // The units on their own.
    print(2h);      // 7200
    print(21m);     // 1260
    print(37s);     // 37
    print(37);      // 37: a bare number is seconds, and still the integer 37

    // Run together, which is the spelling the builtin exists for.
    print(2h21m37); // 7200 + 1260 + 37
    print(1h30m);   // 5400
    print(59s);     // 59
    print(60m);     // 3600, not 60: the unit decides
    print(24h);     // 86400
    print(1h0m0s);  // 3600, and every part of it is written out
    print(0h0m0);   // 0

    // Upper case is accepted, since `0X` and `1E3` are.
    print(1H);
    print(1M);
    print(1S);

    // Underscores separate digits inside a component, as they do in any number.
    print(1_000s);
    print(2h1_0m);

    // A duration is an integer, so it is an ordinary operand.
    print(2h + 1);
    print(1h / 1m);   // 60
    var d = 2h21m37;
    print(d);

    // Zero waits return at once, in every spelling.
    hold(0);
    hold(0s);
    hold(0h0m0);
    hold(0h0m0s);
    print("held zero");

    // One real second, so the runtime call itself is covered. It is the only
    // thing in this file that takes any time.
    hold(1);
    print("held one second");
    return 0;
}
